<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.2. Solving a Sturm-Liouville problem</title>
<p>
  <alert>MTH 401</alert>
</p>



  <definition xml:id="def-sturm-liouville">
    <statement>
      <p>
      A general <term>Sturm-Liouville problem</term> has the form 
      <md>
        <mrow>\ds \frac{d}{dx}\left[p(x)\frac{du}{dx}\right] + q(x)u \amp = -\lambda \omega(x)u </mrow>
        <mrow> u(a) \amp = c_1</mrow>
        <mrow> u(b) \amp = c_2</mrow>
      </md>
      where <m>a, b</m> are the extreme boundary conditions of <m>x</m>. Solving a problem means finding the 
     the <term>eigenvalues</term>, <m>\lambda</m>, that correspond to non-trivial solutions, and then find the 
      corresponding solutions or <term>eigenfunctions</term> <m>u = u(x)</m> associated to each <m>\lambda</m>.
    </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

    
  <remark>
    <p>
      We'll mostly focus on the simpler form of the problem: (not always called <m>X</m>!):
      <md>
        <mrow> X'' \amp = -kX </mrow>
        <mrow>X(a) \amp = 0 </mrow>
        <mrow>X(b) \amp = 0. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>


  <exercise>
  <introduction>
    <p>Consider the homogeneous heat equation when the ends
are insulated, i.e.
<md>
<mrow>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t} \amp  =   6\frac{\partial^2
                                      u}{\partial^2 x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>\ds \pd{u}{x}(0, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
<mrow>\ds \pd{u}{x}(10, t) \amp  =   0</mrow>
</md></p>
  </introduction>

    <task workspace="1.8in">
      <statement>
      <p>First apply separation of variables;  that is, 
  look for a solution with the form
  <m> u(x,t) = X(x) T(t).</m>
  This will enable us to write the partial
  derivatives of <m>u</m> in terms of simpler ordinary derivatives of <m>X</m> and <m>T</m>.
  Find <m>\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}, \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial^2 x^2}</m> in terms of <m>X(x), T(t)</m>, 
  substitute into the heat equation,
  and rearrange the equation so that all occurrences of <m>x</m> are on one
  side and all the occurrences of <m>t</m> are on the other.  This leads to
  a separation constant <m>-k</m>.  You should then find (a) an ordinary
  differential equation for <m>T</m>, (b) an ordinary differential equation
  for <m>X</m>, and (c) two boundary conditions for <m>X</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        Separation of variables: assume the solution separates as 
        <m>{u(x,t) = X(x)T(t)}</m> and try to find it by plugging it in. 
        Then 
        <m>
          {\pd{u}{t} = XT'}</m>,  <m>{\pdtwo{u}{x} = X''T}</m>, 
        so the PDE says <m>{XT' = 6X''T'}</m>. Dividing by <m>6XT</m> we rearrange to get 
        <md>
          \frac16 \frac{T'}{T} = \frac{X''}{X}
        </md>
        and since a function of time only equals a function of space only, 
        they both must be equal to a constant, <m>-k</m>.  So we get two 
        ordinary differential equations:
        <md>
          T' = -6kT, \quad \quad X''=-kX.
        </md>
        Boundary conditions when plugging in our solution <m>{u(x,t)=X(x)T(t)}</m>, 
        we have 
        <md>
          \pd{u}{x}(0,t) = 0 = X'(0)T(t)
        </md>
        and since <m>T(t)</m> is not <m>0</m> then <m>X'(0)=0</m>. 
        Similarly
        <md>
          \pd{u}{x}(10,t) = 0 = X'(10)T(t)
        </md>
        so <m>X'(10)=0</m>.  
        
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>


  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>The differential equation and boundary conditions for <m>X</m> are
  called a <em>Sturm-Liouville</em> problem.  As in the video, look for
  eigenvalues <m>k</m> and eigenfunctions <m>X</m> by considering</p>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 1</term>:  <m>k &lt; 0</m> so that <m>k=-m^2</m>.  The differential equation
  <m>X''=m^2X</m> has solutions that are linear combinations of a growing
  and decaying exponential.
    </p>
  </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        So <m>X(x) = Ae^{mx}+Be^{-mx}</m> and thus <m>{X'(x) = Ame^{mx}-Bme^{-mx}}</m>.
        Boundary Conditions (B.C.):
        <md>
          X'(0)=0 = Am-Bm = m(A-B).
        </md>
        By assumption, <m>m\ne 0</m>, so <m>A = B</m>.  The 2nd B.C.:
        <md>
          X'(10) = 0 = Ame^{10m}-Ame^{-10m} = Am\left(e^{10m}-\frac{1}{e^{10m}}\right)
        </md>
        but the only way <m>e^{10m}-\frac{1}{e^{10m}} = 0</m> is if <m>m=0</m>, which 
        again cannot happen in this case.  So <m>A = 0</m> and thus 
        <m>B = 0</m> and thus <m>X(x) = 0</m> (and thus <m>{u(x,t)=X(x)T(t) = 0}</m>) 
        and that helps us not at all.  So there are no negative eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </solution>
</task>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 2</term>:  <m>k = 0</m> so the differential equation for <m>X</m> is
  <m>X''=0</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        So <m>X'' = 0 \implies X'(x) = A \implies X(x) = Ax+B</m>.  B.C.:
        <m>{X'(0)=0 = A}</m> so <m>{A=0}</m>.  Similarly
        <m>{X'(10)=0=A}</m>.  No new info there.  So <m>B</m> can 
        be any constant really, and so we'll let <m>B = 1</m> for simplicity. 
        Then <m>k=0</m> is an eigenvalue with eigenfunction <m>X(x) = 1</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
</task>
<task workspace="1.8in">
  <statement>
    <p>
      <term>Case 3</term>:  <m>k &gt; 0</m> so that <m>k=m^2</m>.  The differential equation
  <m>X''=-m^2X</m> has solutions that are linear combinations of a sine and
  a cosine function.
    </p>
  </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        So <m>X(x) = A\sin(mx)+B\cos(mx)</m> and thus 
        <m>{X'(x) = Am\cos(mx)-Bm\sin(mx)}</m>. B.C.:
        <m> {X'(0) = 0 = Am \implies A = 0}</m>.  Also
        <md>
          X'(10) = 0 = -Bm\sin(10m).
        </md>
        Now the fun part.  Choose <m>m</m> so that 
        <m>{\sin(10m) = 0}</m>, otherwise you have a trivial solution
        which we don't care about.  The sine function is 0 when evaluated 
        at <m>{n\pi}</m> for integers <m>n</m>, so we will take 
        <m>{10m = n\pi}</m>.  Since we only care about <m>m\gt 0</m> in this case
        then this is true for <m>n=1,2,\ldots</m>. Then
        <md>
          k = m^2 = \left(\frac{n\pi}{10}\right)^2
        </md>
        are the eigenvalues.  The corresponding eigenfunctions (we know 
        nothing about <m>B</m> so it can be any constant, we'll let <m>{B = 1}</m>)
        are <md>
          X_n(x) = \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{10}\right).
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
</task>
    
    </statement>

  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Summarize your findings by stating the eigenvalues and
  eigenfunctions to this Sturm-Liouville problem:
  <md>
  \begin{aligned}
    X'' \amp  = -kX \\
    X'(0) \amp  = 0 \\
    X'(L) \amp  = 0 \\
  \end{aligned}
  </md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        (We'll add subscripts for ease of naming:) Eigenvalue <m>k=0</m> with eigenfunction <m>{X_0(x) = 1}</m>. And 
        eigenvalues <m>{\ds k = \left(\frac{n\pi}{L}\right)^2}</m> with 
        eigenfunctions <m>{X_n(x)= \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)}</m> for 
        <m>{n = 1, 2, \ldots}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>When <m>L=10</m>, sketch the graphs of the eigenfunctions
  corresponding to the three smallest eigenvalues.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">

      <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%">
        <stack>
          <p>
            <m>k=0,</m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m> X_0(x) = 1</m>
          </p>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>The first eigenfunction X=1 which corresponds to the smallest 
              eigenvalue k=0.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-2-2-a">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                   <definition>f(x)=cos(0*pi*x/10)</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1.5,11,1.45)">
                      <axes decorations="no" 
                      xlabel="x" ylabel="X_0(x)"/>
                      <label anchor="(0,72.5)" alignment="w"><m>72.5</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(10,0)" alignment="s"><m>10</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="w" offset="(-5,0)"><m>1</m></label>
                      <graph function="f" domain="(0,10)"/>
                      <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                      <point p="(10,f(10))"/>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>
        </stack>
        <stack>
          <p>
            <m>n=1 </m></p><p><m>\implies k=(\frac{\pi}{10})^2, </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>X_1(x) = \cos\left(\frac{\pi x}{10}\right)</m>
          </p>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>The second eigenfunction X=cos(pi/10) which corresponds to the 2nd smallest 
              eigenvalue k=(pi/10)^2.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-2-2-b">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                   <definition>f(x)=cos(1*pi*x/10)</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1.5,11,1.45)">
                      <axes decorations="no" 
                      xlabel="x" ylabel="X_0(x)"/>
                      <label anchor="(0,72.5)" alignment="w"><m>72.5</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(10,0)" alignment="s"><m>10</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="w" offset="(-5,0)"><m>1</m></label>
                      <graph function="f" domain="(0,10)"/>
                      <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                      <point p="(10,f(10))"/>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>
        </stack>
        <stack>
          <p>
            <m>n=2</m></p><p><m> \implies k=(\frac{2\pi}{10})^2, </m>
          </p>
          <p>
            <m>X_2(x) = \cos\left(\frac{2\pi x}{10}\right)</m>
          </p>
          <image>
            <shortdescription>The third eigenfunction X=cos(2*pi/10) which corresponds to the 3rd smallest 
              eigenvalue k=(2*pi/10)^2.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-401-2-2-c">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                   <definition>f(x)=cos(2*pi*x/10)</definition>
                    <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1.5,11,1.45)">
                      <axes decorations="no" 
                      xlabel="x" ylabel="X_0(x)"/>
                      <label anchor="(0,72.5)" alignment="w"><m>72.5</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(10,0)" alignment="s"><m>10</m></label>
                      <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="w" offset="(-5,0)"><m>1</m></label>
                      <graph function="f" domain="(0,10)"/>
                      <point p="(0,f(0))"/>
                      <point p="(10,f(10))"/>
                    </coordinates>
                </diagram>
            </prefigure>
           </image>
        </stack>
      </sidebyside>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>For each eigenvalue, solve the differential equation for <m>T(t)</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        For <m>k=0</m>: <m>T' = 0 \implies T = constant</m>, so choose 
        <m>{T(t) = 1}</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>\ds k = \left(\frac{n\pi}{10}\right)^2</m>: 
        <m>{\ds T' = -6\left(\frac{n\pi}{10}\right)^2T}</m>
        so <m>{T(t) = e^{-6 (\frac{n\pi}{10})^2 t}}</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Write the general solution to the original heat equation with boundary
  conditions given on the first page.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        Since <m>u(x,t) = X(x)T(t)</m> and <m>X_0T_0</m>, <m>X_1T_1</m>, ... 
        are all solutions, then superposition says a linear combination of them is the 
        general solution:
        <md>
          u(x,t) = A_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n e^{-6(\frac{n\pi}{10})^2 t}\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{10}\right).
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
